Volatility-Regime-Switching Strategy — Technical Report
This report covers the BTC/ETH regime-switching strategy end to end: how it decides when to trade, what it trades (Momentum vs. Reversal), how positions are sized and closed, and — since you asked specifically — exactly what changed across the four iterations of the script and why each change was made.

1. What the strategy is doing, conceptually
The strategy consumes your volatility model's predicted trailing volatility (pred_vol) for each candle and uses it to answer one question first: is the market currently calm or chaotic, relative to its own recent history? That answer routes every candle into one of two fundamentally different trading engines:
Momentum engine — active when volatility is not unusually elevated. Trades breakouts/trend continuation, using VWAP and RSI.
Reversal engine — active when volatility is unusually elevated (a "chaotic" regime). Trades mean-reversion, fading extreme moves back toward a moving average, using Bollinger Bands.
This is intentional: momentum strategies tend to work in orderly trending markets, while sharp/volatile markets are more prone to overextension and snapback. Rather than run one strategy everywhere, the code switches which strategy is "live" based on a rolling z-score of predicted volatility.
1.1 The regime switch (Phase 1)
pred_sigma = sqrt(clip(pred_vol, 0) / 6)          # convert model's 6-candle realized vol into a per-candle sigma
z = rolling_zscore(pred_vol, lookback=672, min_periods=96)   # 672 candles = 7 days of 15m bars
regime = "reversal"           if z >= z_threshold (0.8)
       = "momentum"           if z <  z_threshold
       = "insufficient_data"  if z is NaN (not enough history yet -> hold cash)

The z-score lookback carries over from validation into test (via prior_pred), so the test split's early candles aren't left with an artificially short warmup window — the rolling statistics are computed over the concatenation of both splits, and only the test-split slice is returned.
1.2 Momentum engine (Phase 2)
Indicators used: VWAP (anchored daily, 96 candles) and RSI with adaptive thresholds (a rolling mean ± k·std of RSI's own recent history, replacing old fixed 70/30 levels).
Entry:
Long: close > VWAP AND RSI >= adaptive_upper_threshold
Short: close < VWAP AND RSI <= adaptive_lower_threshold
This requires price to already be on the "right side" of the day's volume-weighted average price and RSI to be flagging an unusually strong (relative to its own recent history) directional push — i.e., trade with a confirmed, currently-strong trend rather than any VWAP crossing.
Exit: covered in detail in the version-by-version breakdown below, since this is one of the parts that changed the most. In the final version, momentum exits fire the first time RSI pulls back off its running peak (long) or trough (short) since entry — i.e., the first sign the trend is stalling — once a minimum hold period has passed.
1.3 Reversal engine (Phase 3)
Indicators used (final version): Bollinger Bands (rolling SMA ± k standard deviations of close), ATR (for stop-loss sizing only), price gradient (deceleration filter), and a "choppiness" gate (fraction of recent candles moving in the same direction).
Entry:
Short: close > bb_upper (price has broken above the upper band — potentially overextended)
Long: close < bb_lower (price has broken below the lower band)
Two optional confirmation filters can be layered on top (both are asset-specific toggles — see Section 3):
Gradient deceleration filter: only fade the breakout if the trailing price gradient is already decelerating (i.e., the move is losing steam), rather than fading a still-accelerating move.
Choppiness filter: only fade the breakout if the recent run of candles was NOT overwhelmingly one-directional (>80% one-sided is treated as a genuine trend, not a fadeable spike).
Exit: a hard ATR-based stop-loss (unconditional), plus — as of the second iteration — either a snapback to the band's mid-line OR the volatility z-score itself reverting back below the regime threshold (meaning the "reversal regime" thesis that justified the trade no longer holds). See the version breakdown for the exact evolution.
1.4 Position sizing (shared by both engines)
raw_size = target_risk_frac / pred_sigma        # inverse-volatility sizing: less confident/more volatile -> smaller size
position = direction * min(raw_size, max_leverage_per_asset)

This is volatility-targeted position sizing: the higher the model's predicted volatility, the smaller the position, so that (in expectation) each trade risks roughly the same fraction of capital regardless of how turbulent the market currently is. A hard leverage cap (max_leverage_per_asset, default 1.5x) prevents sizing from blowing up when predicted volatility is very low.
1.5 The state machine (Phase 4)
Each asset (BTC, ETH) runs its own independent state machine, one candle at a time:
If a position is open: check that position's own locked-in engine's exit conditions first (a reversal trade is never exited using momentum exit logic, and vice versa — the entry regime is "locked in" for the life of the trade).
If flat: look at the current candle's regime label and check that regime's entry conditions.
An important, deliberately-designed nuance carries through every version: exit triggers are unconditional (they fire purely on the technical condition, regardless of P&L), but the transacted size at exit is price-conditional. Concretely: closing a long only actually sells (momentum_exit_close_frac, default 90%) of the position if the current price is above entry price; if price is below entry, the code marks the trade as closed in its bookkeeping (state resets to flat) but transacts 0% — no shares are actually sold. The same logic applies (with close_frac=1.0, i.e. full close) to reversal trades on their snapback/vol-reversion exit, though the reversal stop-loss always fully closes regardless of this gate. This is an unusual design choice worth being aware of: the state can go flat while the position array can retain a stale non-zero "closed but not transacted" entry recorded as trade_units. The code is explicit and consistent about this, but it means "signal == EXIT" does not always mean "quantity changed."

2. Version-by-version changes
You supplied four successive versions of the script. Here is exactly what changed at each step, and the rationale behind it (as documented in the code's own comments, which I've cross-checked against the actual logic diffs).
Version 1 → Version 2: "Additional exit trigger for reversal + RSI lookback shortened to 5"
(a) Momentum exit: VWAP invalidation removed
Before: momentum exits fired on either VWAP invalidation (price crosses back over VWAP against the position) or RSI peak-reversal (first pullback off the running RSI extreme since entry).
After: VWAP invalidation is removed entirely. RSI peak-reversal is now the only momentum exit trigger.
Why this likely helps: VWAP invalidation is a much looser, earlier-firing condition — price only needs to cross back over a single reference line, which can happen on ordinary chop even while the broader trend (and RSI) is still intact. Removing it lets winning momentum trades run longer, exiting only when momentum itself (via RSI) shows genuine exhaustion, rather than being stopped out by a routine VWAP wiggle.
(b) Reversal exit: added a volatility-regime-reversion trigger
Before: reversal trades exited on stop-loss (unconditional) OR snapback to the Keltner mid-line (after min_hold_candles, and profit-gated for BTC).
After: a third exit condition is added — vol_regime_reverted: once min_hold_candles has passed, the trade also exits if the rolling volatility z-score drops back below z_threshold. This is OR'd with the snapback condition (either alone is sufficient to fire).
Why this likely helps: the entire premise of a reversal trade is "volatility is elevated, so this move is more likely to be an overextension than a genuine trend." If the z-score itself reverts back to "calm" territory before price ever snaps back to the band mid-line, the original thesis for holding the trade has quietly expired — continuing to hold it is now trading on stale reasoning. Exiting here converts a class of otherwise-stuck/losing setups (where volatility calmed down but price never snapped back) into earlier, cleaner exits.
(c) RSI lookback shortened
The CLI/pipeline default for rsi_n (the Wilder-smoothing period for RSI) is reduced from 14 (original TA-Lib-style default) down through 7 to 5 across these versions, ending at 5 by the second version's argparse default (the run_volatility_regime_pipeline function default stayed at 7, but the CLI entry point — which is what actually runs — defaults to 5).
Why this likely helps: a shorter RSI period makes RSI more reactive to very recent price action. Since RSI here drives both the momentum entry gate (requiring RSI to already be at an adaptive extreme) and the exit trigger (first pullback off the running peak/trough), a faster RSI reaches its peak/trough sooner and reacts to a stall in momentum more promptly — consistent with the exit-timing goal in (a): "exit near the actual top/bottom of the trend" rather than waiting for a slower-moving average to catch up.
The RSI adaptive-threshold multiplier rsi_threshold_k is also raised (1.9 → 2.0 in the CLI default), which widens the adaptive band slightly — a small offsetting adjustment likely made to compensate for a faster/noisier RSI by requiring a slightly larger deviation from its own rolling mean before flagging a trend.
Version 2 → Version 3: "Bollinger Bands instead of Keltner Channels"
Before: the reversal engine's bands were Keltner Channels — an EMA(20) midline ± a multiple of ATR(14). Bands widen/narrow based on true range volatility.
After: the reversal engine's bands are Bollinger Bands — a rolling SMA(20) midline ± a multiple (2.0) of the rolling standard deviation of closing price over the same window. Bands widen/narrow based on closing-price dispersion, which is a related but distinct concept from ATR-based true range.
ATR is still computed and still drives the stop-loss distance — that part of the reversal engine is untouched; only the band definition used for entry/snapback-exit changed.
Why this might help/what it changes in practice:
Bollinger Bands react purely to how dispersed closing prices have been, while Keltner Channels react to the true range (which includes gaps and intracandle spikes even if the close is unchanged). In a 15-minute crypto series, this means Bollinger Bands can be somewhat "tighter" during quiet-but-choppy periods (many small closes, low ATR-relevant range) and looser during strong sustained directional moves (where consecutive closes drift steadily, inflating the standard deviation).
Practically, this changes which breakouts count as "extension enough to fade." Since the reversal engine is already gated to only activate when the independently-computed volatility z-score says the regime is elevated, switching the band type changes the sensitivity of entries within that regime, rather than changing when the regime itself is active.
This is a hypothesis about mechanism, not a guaranteed improvement — Bollinger vs. Keltner is a classic, empirically-dependent choice in technical trading; whichever produces better precision on cutting fades from genuine spikes (vs. genuine trends) on your specific BTC/ETH 15m data is the one that will show up as improved backtest performance. The comments in the code don't state a backtested rationale beyond the swap itself, so treat this specific change as the most "try it and see" of the four.
Version 3 → Version 4: "Transaction fees added"
Two separate places changed, both driven by the same fee_bps (basis points) input, previously accepted as a parameter but functionally unused (always trading_cost = 0.0):
(a) Position sizing now grosses up for fees
fee_rate = fee_bps / 10000
fee_size_scale = 1 / (1 - fee_rate)
raw_size = (target_risk_frac / sigma) * fee_size_scale

Every new entry's requested size (before the leverage cap) is inflated by 1/(1 - fee_rate). With fee_bps=0 this is a no-op (fee_size_scale = 1.0). With a nonzero fee, this ensures the position's net-of-fee risk still matches target_risk_frac, rather than under-shooting the intended risk target once fees are subtracted.
(b) Fees are now actually charged in the backtest
trading_cost = fee_rate * df["trade_units"].abs()
strategy_return = gross_return - trading_cost

Previously trading_cost was hardcoded to 0.0 regardless of fee_bps — meaning every prior backtest (versions 1–3) was, in fact, a frictionless/zero-fee backtest no matter what fee value was passed in. This version fixes that: the fee is charged on abs(trade_units) for every candle where a transaction actually occurred — which correctly captures entries, full exits, and partial closes (since trade_units already tracks the true transacted quantity via the price-conditional exit-size logic described in Section 1.5, not just "did the position change to/from zero"). run_combined_backtest was updated in parallel so the 50/50 BTC/ETH combined equity curve also reflects fees on each leg independently before combining.
Why this matters for interpreting your results: any performance comparison between versions 1–3 and version 4 needs to account for the fact that only version 4 has ever actually deducted trading costs from the equity curve. If your earlier backtests looked strong, part of that could simply be the absence of fee drag rather than genuinely better signal quality — version 4 is the first version whose Sharpe/return/drawdown numbers reflect real-world cost of trading (at whatever fee_bps you pass in; the default remains 0, so you must explicitly set a nonzero value to see this take effect).

