4. Summary of the net effect of all four changes together
Taken together, the four rounds of changes push the strategy in a consistent direction:
Momentum trades are held longer and exited more precisely (RSI-peak-reversal only, faster RSI) — cutting an early, loose exit trigger (VWAP invalidation) in favor of a single, more trend-exhaustion-specific one.
Reversal trades get an additional, thesis-aware exit (volatility regime reverting) — reducing the number of trades that sit open after the original justification for entering has already faded.
The reversal engine's band definition changed from range/ATR-based (Keltner) to dispersion-based (Bollinger) — a mechanism-level change to which breakouts count as fade-worthy, whose net effect is data-dependent rather than obviously one-directional.
Real transaction costs are now modeled, both in position sizing (grossing up for fee drag) and in the backtest P&L itself — meaning this is the first version where reported returns/Sharpe/drawdown reflect actual trading frictions rather than a frictionless idealization.
If you're comparing backtest metrics across these versions, keep points 3 and 4 especially in mind: point 3 is a genuine strategy-logic change that could help or hurt depending on your data, and point 4 means any version-4 numbers are not directly comparable to versions 1–3 unless you also re-run 1–3 with the same nonzero fee_bps and the (previously broken) fee-charging line manually patched in.
